---
/**
 * Portfolio four: the six holdings of portfolio three plus one fund that borrows inside
 * itself to hold managed futures on top of stocks.
 *
 * The six warnings come before the case rather than after it, because most headline
 * numbers on this page belong to a 25% arm and the published weight is 30%. The one
 * matched 30-against-25 test (Experiment 016f) clears its floor only at the trend return
 * the 1990–2026 panel paid; at the forward figure it does not. Nothing here is a
 * promotion: against its borrowed control the portfolio sits under the resolution of the
 * test that produced it, and the financing cost that would settle the question is not
 * disclosed.
 *
 * The drawdown ladder is a static table rather than a selector. Four rows read at a
 * glance, and the site's islands exist only where a reader has to type something.
 */
import Breakout from "~/components/Breakout.astro";
import Figure from "~/components/Figure.astro";
import Base from "~/layouts/Base.astro";

const HOLDING_COLUMNS = ["Ticker", "Fund", "Weight", "Fee", "Cost after lending income"] as const;

/**
 * The drawdown ladder, as the research page prints it: the money in RSST that a stated
 * worst loss supports once the loss assumed is widened for today's valuation. The rows
 * are quoted, never computed here; the `drawdown-ladder` record beneath the table carries
 * the provenance. The last row is where the research pages place the published weight,
 * and it is an implied figure rather than a row the ladder was run at.
 */
const LADDER_COLUMNS = ["Worst loss you would sit through", "Money in RSST"] as const;

const LADDER = [
  { tolerance: "−30%", weight: "11.0%" },
  { tolerance: "−40%", weight: "14.9%" },
  { tolerance: "−50%", weight: "19.1%" },
  { tolerance: "−60%", weight: "23.7%" },
  { tolerance: "about −70%, implied", weight: "30%, as published" },
] as const;

const HOLDINGS = [
  {
    ticker: "RSST",
    name: "Return Stacked U.S. Stocks & Managed Futures ETF",
    weight: "30%",
    fee: "0.99%",
    net: "not audited",
  },
  {
    ticker: "VTI",
    name: "Vanguard Morningstar Total Stock Market ETF",
    weight: "19%",
    fee: "0.03%",
    net: "0.012%",
  },
  { ticker: "VXUS", name: "Vanguard Total International Stock ETF", weight: "16%", fee: "0.05%", net: "0.014%" },
  { ticker: "VTV", name: "Vanguard Value ETF", weight: "15%", fee: "0.03%", net: "0.027%" },
  { ticker: "AVDV", name: "Avantis International Small Cap Value ETF", weight: "10%", fee: "0.36%", net: "0.300%" },
  {
    ticker: "IDMO",
    name: "Invesco S&P International Developed Momentum ETF",
    weight: "5%",
    fee: "0.25%",
    net: "0.226%",
  },
  { ticker: "AVES", name: "Avantis Emerging Markets Value ETF", weight: "5%", fee: "0.36%", net: "0.292%" },
] as const;
---

<Base
  title="The same, plus a holding that does not move with stocks"
  description="Seven funds, one of which borrows inside itself to hold managed futures on top of stocks. The largest bet on this site and the least settled."
>
  <div class="prose">
    <h1 class="font-serif text-4xl tracking-[-0.02em] text-ink">
      Four. The same, plus a holding that does not move with stocks
    </h1>

    <p class="lead">What this adds to <a href="/portfolios/value-lean/">portfolio three</a>, what it costs, and
    six things to know before holding it. This is the least settled page on the site.</p>

    <h2>What it holds</h2>
  </div>

  <Breakout scroll class="my-8" label="The seven holdings, their fees and their costs" maxWidth="50rem">
    <table role="table" class="stack-table w-full border-collapse text-base sm:min-w-[46rem]">
      <caption class="sr-only">
        The seven funds this portfolio holds, each with its weight, its fee and what it costs after lending income
      </caption>
      <thead role="rowgroup">
        <tr role="row" class="border-b border-rule-strong text-left">
          {
            HOLDING_COLUMNS.map((heading, i) => (
              <th role="columnheader" scope="col" class:list={["eyebrow py-2 pr-4", i >= 2 && "text-right"]}>
                {heading}
              </th>
            ))
          }
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody role="rowgroup" class="text-ink-muted">
        {
          HOLDINGS.map((holding) => (
            <tr role="row" class="border-b border-rule last:border-0">
              <th role="rowheader" scope="row" class="py-2 pr-4 text-left font-normal">
                {holding.ticker}
              </th>
              <td role="cell" data-label={HOLDING_COLUMNS[1]} class="py-2 pr-4">
                {holding.name}
              </td>
              <td role="cell" data-label={HOLDING_COLUMNS[2]} data-numeric class="py-2 pr-4 text-right">
                {holding.weight}
              </td>
              <td role="cell" data-label={HOLDING_COLUMNS[3]} data-numeric class="py-2 pr-4 text-right">
                {holding.fee}
              </td>
              <td role="cell" data-label={HOLDING_COLUMNS[4]} data-numeric class="py-2 pr-4 text-right">
                {holding.net}
              </td>
            </tr>
          ))
        }
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </Breakout>

  <div class="prose">
    <p>Weighted, about 0.38% a year in fees and about 0.36% after lending income, or roughly $38 and $36 on
    $10,000 as an illustration. RSST&rsquo;s 0.99% has no waiver, so that is both its gross and its net figure.
    Fund facts were read from filings on 17 August 2026.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="prose mt-14">
    <h2>Six things to know before you read any further</h2>

    <p>One test has run these seven funds at 30% in RSST against the same seven at 25%, with the five points
    otherwise sitting in VTI. Over 427 months to May 2026 the gap between them was
    <Figure id="matched-thirty-over-twenty-five" inline showUnit={false} /> a year in favour of 30%, and the smallest gap
    that test could see was 0.31, so it is the one whole-portfolio comparison on this site that clears its own
    resolution. It is a result about borrowing at what trend paid over those years, 10.98 points a year over cash.
    At the 4.07 points we would guess going forward the same gap is about 0.18, under what the test could see,
    and at 1.80 about 0.07. Every other headline number below belongs to the 25% version; the 30% one scored
    2.50 points against its borrowed control, plausibly −0.15 to 5.26, against a smallest visible gap of 3.38,
    which settles nothing either way.</p>

    <p><Figure id="active-variance-concentration" inline showUnit={false} /> of this portfolio&rsquo;s active
    risk sits in RSST alone. Not 98% of the money, which is 30%. Almost everything that makes this portfolio
    different from an index fund is one fund from a small firm.</p>

    <p>Of the <a href="/evidence/trend-following/">managed-futures exchange-traded funds</a> filing in July 2019,
    <Figure id="managed-futures-attrition" inline showUnit={false} /> had stopped filing by the end of 2025.
    Thirteen of twenty-five, and a lower bound, because a fund that never filed at all cannot appear in the
    count.</p>

    <p>RSST&rsquo;s measured exposure rests on 31 months of filings, roughly one kind of market weather. The fund
    is under three years old.</p>

    <p>It holds no bonds. That is a choice made here rather than an oversight, and how much you keep in
    <a href="/evidence/what-actually-diversifies/">bonds or cash</a> outside it remains yours to decide.</p>

    <p>The fund does not disclose what its borrowing costs and files 0.00% of interest expense, as does every
    fund in its family. That cost decides whether the whole idea is worth anything, and nobody has measured
    it.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="prose mt-14">
    <h2>Why each piece is there</h2>

    <p>The six holdings from <a href="/portfolios/value-lean/">portfolio three</a> do the same jobs they did
    there. One caveat carries over: IDMO is the weakest of them and the first to drop if you want a simpler
    portfolio. <a href="/evidence/value-and-factors/">Momentum</a> has the widest gap on this site between what
    an idea pays on paper and what anyone has collected, 9.48% a year against 1.86% delivered by real funds
    before fees, and it turns over 105% of itself a year against 4% for the value fund beside it.</p>

    <p>RSST is the addition. It holds about a dollar of US stocks and about a dollar of managed futures for every
    dollar you put in, borrowing inside the fund to do it.
    <a href="/evidence/trend-following/">Managed futures follow price trends up and down</a> across stock
    indexes, bonds, currencies and commodities. Because of the borrowing you never sell stocks to buy them, so
    the question becomes whether they add anything on top.</p>

    <p>The record of funds that borrow inside themselves is worth knowing before you buy one. The two best-known
    funds that use borrowing to hold stocks and bonds together both did worse than a plain 60/40 mix in 2022,
    which was the one year the idea was supposed to prove itself: one lost 25.72% and the other 43.17%. The
    second of those advertises a 0.59% fee. Its real all-in cost is 2.94%, and its five-year return after tax was
    −0.57% a year.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="prose mt-14">
    <h2>What it is compared against, and what the comparison showed</h2>

    <p>The comparison is a cheap global index fund borrowed to the same total exposure and charged the same
    interest, over 427 months to May 2026. Setting this portfolio against an index fund that borrows nothing
    would credit the borrowing as if it were skill.</p>

    <p>Against that borrowed index fund, the 25% version came to 2.20 points a year ahead, plausibly anywhere
    from 0.05 to 4.57. The smallest gap the test could see was 2.83 points, so the result sits below the
    resolution of the test that produced it. Separating the two would take about 59 years, and the specification
    said so before the test ran.</p>

    <p>What is established is narrower. The exposure is real: the fund delivered about 0.68 of a dollar of trend
    per dollar of capital, in a range of 0.41 to 0.96, measured from its own filed returns.</p>

    <p>Its <a href="/evidence/what-actually-diversifies/">relationship with stocks</a> is thinner evidence than
    it looks, and this is the property you would be buying the fund for. Across the full period that relationship
    measures slightly negative in three separate sources built from different data. Inside crisis months one
    measurement puts it much more strongly negative, but those months are four episodes rather than four
    independent years, and a longer run of data reaching back to 1934, built differently, finds no relationship
    at all. Nobody has reconciled the two.</p>

    <p>What is not established is the average return, anywhere, by anyone.</p>

    <p>One thing this kind of holding has clearly done: through the ten years to February 2009, when US stocks
    lost 2.55% a year, a 30% trend holding added about 9.5 points a year above the stocks it displaced, turning
    that decade into roughly +0.05% a year. In ordinary decades it added 0.21 points. One decade, in one set of
    data, and that is the whole case.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="prose mt-14">
    <h2>How sure we are</h2>

    <p>We can&rsquo;t tell. The measured gap is smaller than what the test could see, the fund is under three
    years old, and the one number that would decide it, what the borrowing costs, is not disclosed.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="prose mt-14">
    <h2>What it feels like when it is losing</h2>

    <p>The tested version fell 50.3% at its worst, against 64.6% for a control that borrows as much and 52.7% for
    a cheap control that borrows nothing. It spent 42 months under water: three and a half years of opening a
    statement and seeing less than you had at the peak.</p>

    <p>The way this holding hurts in particular is worse. A borrowed trend holding has run
    <Figure id="trend-drawdown" inline showUnit={false} /> the stocks it displaced over 11.2 years, and did not
    recover inside the window. Eleven years of paying 0.99% a year for a fund that did nothing but cost you
    money, while stocks rose and everyone who owned nothing clever did better than you did.</p>

    <p>If trend keeps paying what we guess it pays, about
    <Figure id="abandonment-30" inline showUnit={false} /> of simulated thirty-year paths end with the holder
    selling. If it has stopped paying entirely,
    <Figure id="abandonment-30-dead" inline showUnit={false} /> do. Those risks do not cancel: whether you can
    hold it and whether it pays are a bet on the same unknown.</p>

    <p>Since it launched in September 2023 RSST has returned 17.17% a year against 21.50% for the S&amp;P 500.
    Thirty-five months settles nothing about a holding whose whole purpose is the years stocks lose. It is what
    the statement says, and evidence in neither direction.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="prose mt-14">
    <h2>What would make us drop it</h2>

    <p>The fund closing or merging, which happened to
    <Figure id="managed-futures-attrition" inline showUnit={false} /> of its category in six and a half years.
    Delivered exposure falling below what the filings claim. The financing cost being disclosed and turning out
    to be large. And the matched test, now run once: these seven funds at 30% against the same seven at 25% came
    out <Figure id="matched-thirty-over-twenty-five" inline showUnit={false} /> ahead at what trend paid over
    the window, and about 0.18 at what we would guess it pays from here. The published 30% stands for someone
    who keeps contributing through a long stretch of the fund doing nothing; for someone who would sell it in
    that stretch, 25% wins against 30%, and a portfolio held beats a better one abandoned.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="prose mt-14">
    <h2 id="worst-loss">How much to hold depends on the loss you could sit through</h2>

    <p>Nobody has told us the worst loss they would sit through, and this site does not pick one for you. What it
    does instead is show the ladder. US stocks bought at a cyclically adjusted price-earnings ratio above 30, which
    is where the market has stood all year, have run a median real loss of 51.8% over the following fifteen years
    against 36.7% from cheaper entries, with 59.7% of those months spent below the buyer&rsquo;s starting point.
    That rests on fewer than one independent fifteen-year stretch, and it says nothing about what stocks will
    return. What it changes is the loss the borrowing is sized against. Size it against that wider loss, holding
    trend at its published share of the stock exposure, and the money the rule puts in RSST reads off the worst
    loss you state.</p>
  </div>

  <Breakout class="my-8" label="The drawdown ladder" maxWidth="34rem">
    <table role="table" class="stack-table w-full border-collapse text-base">
      <caption class="sr-only">
        The money in RSST that a stated worst loss supports, once the loss assumed is widened for today&rsquo;s
        valuation
      </caption>
      <thead role="rowgroup">
        <tr role="row" class="border-b border-rule-strong text-left">
          {
            LADDER_COLUMNS.map((heading, i) => (
              <th role="columnheader" scope="col" class:list={["eyebrow py-2 pr-4", i >= 1 && "text-right"]}>
                {heading}
              </th>
            ))
          }
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody role="rowgroup" class="text-ink-muted">
        {
          LADDER.map((row) => (
            <tr role="row" class="border-b border-rule last:border-0">
              <th role="rowheader" scope="row" data-numeric class="py-2 pr-4 text-left font-normal">
                {row.tolerance}
              </th>
              <td role="cell" data-label={LADDER_COLUMNS[1]} data-numeric class="py-2 pr-4 text-right">
                {row.weight}
              </td>
            </tr>
          ))
        }
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </Breakout>

  <div class="prose">
    <p>The published 30% is the loosest row. It sits off the bottom of the ladder, and the research behind this
    page puts the worst loss it implies near −70%. Under our own rule a stated tolerance of −60% or tighter moves
    the money in RSST to that row&rsquo;s figure: 23.7% at −60%, 19.1% at −50%. At −50% or tighter the same rule
    also puts ten points of long inflation-linked Treasury bonds, unborrowed, in the traditional retirement
    account, paid for out of VTI and VXUS in proportion, because at today&rsquo;s real yields that is the cheapest
    the protection has been in the record. At −60% or looser it holds none, and none is the default for someone
    who keeps contributing and accepts the borrowing.</p>

    <p>Three limits, each of which cuts both ways. The wider loss is a real figure and the ladder is a nominal
    one, so only the ratio between the two is carried across, on the assumption that the conversion does not
    itself depend on how expensive the market was. The valuation reading has no record of forecasting returns,
    so what survives is what a buyer at these prices had to endure, and nothing about what they earned. And the
    whole rule ends in a number nobody has supplied.</p>

    <Figure id="drawdown-ladder" class="mt-8" />
  </div>

  <div class="prose mt-14">
    <h2>The decision that matters more than this page</h2>

    <p>How much of your money is in stocks at all outweighs everything above. Moving a portfolio from 60% stocks
    to 90% was worth 1.27 points a year, against 4.85 points of variation from one year to the next. That is more
    than every lean on this site combined. Choosing 60/40 is itself a forecast: it says stocks will do better
    than bonds by only about 1.2 points a year. One country, 1963 to 2025, with a modelled ten-year government
    bond, and about <Figure id="equity-step-horizon" inline /> of your own results before you could be 90%
    confident about the step.</p>
  </div>
</Base>
